\description{
Build a typed, tibble-of-tibbles corpus container for bibliometric analysis.
The corpus is the central data structure in scimapR, holding works, authors,
authorships, institutions, sources, references, concepts, embeddings,
provenance, screening decisions, and metadata.
}
\details{
IDs are unique within a corpus; \code{\link[=sm_bind_corpora]{sm_bind_corpora()}} gives the second
corpus new IDs where they collide.

A corpus is a list of tables that share identifiers: \code{work_id} links
every table to \code{works}, \code{author_id} links \code{authorships} to \code{authors},
\code{institution_id} links them to \code{institutions} and \code{source_id} links
\code{works} to \code{sources}. The tables below list, for each element, the
required columns, which the readers and fetchers always write (a value
may be \code{NA}), and the optional columns that some readers and functions
write or read. \code{sm_corpus()} adds the missing required columns of
\code{works} (mostly \code{NA}); it takes the other tables as given.

\strong{\code{works}}: one row per work. Required:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{work_id} \tab character \tab Identifier, unique within the corpus \cr
   \code{doi} \tab character \tab DOI, lower case, without \verb{https://doi.org/} \cr
   \code{title}, \code{abstract} \tab character \tab Title and abstract \cr
   \code{year} \tab integer \tab Publication year \cr
   \code{type} \tab character \tab Document type, as the source names it \cr
   \code{source_id} \tab character \tab The journal or other source (\code{sources}) \cr
   \code{cited_by_count} \tab integer \tab Citations, as the source counts them \cr
   \code{oa_status} \tab character \tab Open-access status \cr
   \code{language} \tab character \tab Language \cr
   \code{pmid}, \code{arxiv_id}, \code{openalex_id} \tab character \tab Other identifiers \cr
   \code{is_retracted} \tab logical \tab Retraction flag \cr
   \code{retraction_date} \tab Date \tab Date of the retraction \cr
   \code{last_refreshed} \tab POSIXct \tab When the record was read or fetched \cr
}


Optional: \code{quartile}, \code{jif_quartile} or \code{sjr_quartile} (journal
quartiles such as \code{"Q1"}, for \code{sm_its(outcome = "share_q1")}); \code{cnci},
\code{fnci}, \code{rcr} and other impact columns (see \code{\link[=sm_its]{sm_its()}}); \code{field} (for
\code{sm_metric_fnci(classification = "manual")}); \code{citation_mature} and
\code{cnci_provisional} (\code{\link[=sm_citation_maturity]{sm_citation_maturity()}}); \code{cluster_id} (the
clustering functions); \code{cluster_label} and \code{cluster_label_basis}, a
cluster's label and how it was made (\code{\link[=sm_cluster_label]{sm_cluster_label()}}). The
OpenAlex readers and \code{\link[=sm_enrich_openalex_impact]{sm_enrich_openalex_impact()}} write these impact
columns:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{fwci} \tab double \tab Field-weighted citation impact from OpenAlex \cr
   \code{citation_percentile} \tab double \tab OpenAlex citation percentile, 0 to 1 \cr
   \code{is_top_10pct} \tab logical \tab Among the 10\% most cited of field and year \cr
   \code{is_top_1pct} \tab logical \tab Among the 1\% most cited of field and year \cr
   \code{citations_as_of} \tab Date \tab Date of these values and the yearly counts \cr
}


The openalexR engine of \code{\link[=sm_fetch_openalex]{sm_fetch_openalex()}} has no percentile, so its
\code{citation_percentile}, \code{is_top_10pct} and \code{is_top_1pct} are \code{NA}.
\code{citations_as_of} is the day of the OpenAlex record's \code{updated_date}, or
of the fetch when the record has none; \code{\link[=sm_read_openalex_json]{sm_read_openalex_json()}} leaves
it \code{NA} for records without \code{updated_date}. \code{\link[=sm_citation_window]{sm_citation_window()}} reads
it to tell complete citation windows from partial ones. A corpus
restored with \code{\link[=sm_corpus_from_tables]{sm_corpus_from_tables()}} can hold it as a day count (days
since 1970-01-01, as SQLite keeps dates) or as ISO 8601 text;
\code{\link[=sm_citation_window]{sm_citation_window()}} and \code{\link[=sm_enrich_openalex_impact]{sm_enrich_openalex_impact()}} read both as
the date they stand for.

\strong{\code{authors}}: one row per author. Required:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{author_id} \tab character \tab Identifier, unique within the corpus \cr
   \code{orcid} \tab character \tab ORCID iD \cr
   \code{display_name} \tab character \tab Name \cr
   \code{display_name_alternatives} \tab list of character \tab Other spellings \cr
   \code{inferred_gender}, \code{gender_method} \tab character \tab Gender and its method \cr
   \code{gender_confidence} \tab double \tab Confidence of \code{inferred_gender} \cr
}


\code{display_name_alternatives} holds, for example, the names as a Web of
Science file writes them, and \code{sm_audit_gender(method = "manual")}
reads the gender columns. Optional: none so far.

\strong{\code{authorships}}: one row per work, author and affiliation, so an author
with several addresses in a work has several rows, which share
\code{position} and \code{is_corresponding}. Required:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{work_id}, \code{author_id} \tab character \tab The work and the author \cr
   \code{position} \tab integer \tab Place in the author list, from 1 (\code{NA}: unknown) \cr
   \code{is_corresponding} \tab logical \tab Corresponding author: see below \cr
   \code{institution_id} \tab character \tab The institution (\code{institutions}) \cr
   \code{raw_affiliation} \tab character \tab The address as the source writes it \cr
   \code{country_code} \tab character \tab ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code \cr
}


\code{is_corresponding} is \code{TRUE} for the authors the source names as
corresponding, \code{FALSE} for the other authors of such a work and \code{NA}
when the source does not say who the corresponding author is.
\code{\link[=sm_read_wos]{sm_read_wos()}} and \code{\link[=as_sm_corpus]{as_sm_corpus()}} read it from the RP field,
\code{\link[=sm_read_openalex_json]{sm_read_openalex_json()}} and \code{\link[=sm_fetch_openalex]{sm_fetch_openalex()}} from OpenAlex (a
work whose authors are all marked \code{false} takes its corresponding
authors from \code{corresponding_author_ids}, and counts as unknown when
that lists none of them), \code{\link[=sm_read_dimensions]{sm_read_dimensions()}} from its Corresponding
Authors column and \code{\link[=sm_fetch_biorxiv]{sm_fetch_biorxiv()}} from the bioRxiv API; the other
readers and fetchers write \code{NA}. Up to scimapR 0.4.0 they flagged the
first author instead. A name that fits several authors of a work
equally (namesakes with the same surname and initials) and that the
source does not tell apart leaves them \code{NA}. Functions that select
corresponding authors take \code{TRUE} only, and the leadership outcome of
\code{\link[=sm_its]{sm_its()}} leaves works whose corresponding author is unknown out of
its shares.

Optional: \code{institution_match}, \code{match_method}, \code{match_signal} and
\code{match_evidence} (\code{\link[=sm_affiliation_match]{sm_affiliation_match()}}); \code{institution_name}
(\code{\link[=sm_attribute_institution]{sm_attribute_institution()}}); \code{email} (exported as \code{EM} by
\code{\link[=sm_to_bibliometrix]{sm_to_bibliometrix()}}).

\strong{\code{institutions}}: one row per institution. Required:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{institution_id} \tab character \tab Identifier, as in \code{authorships} \cr
   \code{ror} \tab character \tab ROR id \cr
   \code{display_name} \tab character \tab Name \cr
   \code{country_code} \tab character \tab ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code \cr
   \code{region}, \code{income_tier} \tab character \tab World region and income group \cr
   \code{type} \tab character \tab Kind of institution, such as education or healthcare \cr
}


Optional: none so far.

\strong{\code{sources}}: one row per journal or other source. Required:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{source_id} \tab character \tab Identifier, as in \code{works} \cr
   \code{issn_l} \tab character \tab Linking ISSN \cr
   \code{issn} \tab list of character \tab All ISSNs of the source \cr
   \code{display_name} \tab character \tab Name \cr
   \code{type} \tab character \tab Kind of source, such as journal \cr
   \code{is_oa} \tab logical \tab Open-access source \cr
   \code{publisher}, \code{publisher_country} \tab character \tab Publisher and its country \cr
}


Optional: \code{abbreviation} and \code{iso_abbreviation} (character; the Web of
Science abbreviations J9 and JI, written by \code{\link[=sm_read_wos]{sm_read_wos()}} and the
bibliometrix conversion and exported by \code{\link[=sm_to_bibliometrix]{sm_to_bibliometrix()}}).

\strong{\code{references}}: one row per cited reference. Required:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{work_id} \tab character \tab The citing work \cr
   \code{ref_index} \tab integer \tab Place in the work's reference list \cr
   \code{cited_work_id} \tab character \tab Cited work in \code{works}; see linking below \cr
   \code{cited_doi} \tab character \tab DOI of the cited work \cr
   \code{cited_raw} \tab character \tab The reference as the source writes it \cr
}


Reference linking: readers, fetchers, \code{\link[=sm_bind_corpora]{sm_bind_corpora()}}, \code{\link[=sm_dedupe]{sm_dedupe()}}
and \code{\link[=sm_link_references]{sm_link_references()}} set \code{cited_work_id} from \code{cited_doi} or an
OpenAlex id in \code{cited_raw} when the cited work is in \code{works}.

Optional: none so far.

\strong{\code{concepts}}: one row per keyword, category or concept of a work.
Required:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{work_id} \tab character \tab The work \cr
   \code{concept_id} \tab character \tab Identifier in its vocabulary \cr
   \code{concept_name} \tab character \tab The keyword, category or concept \cr
   \code{level} \tab integer \tab Level in the hierarchy of the vocabulary \cr
   \code{score} \tab double \tab Weight given by the source \cr
   \code{vocabulary} \tab character \tab One of \code{\link[=sm_concept_vocabularies]{sm_concept_vocabularies()}} \cr
}


For MeSH headings \code{score} is 1 for a major topic and 0 otherwise.
Optional: none so far.

\strong{\code{embeddings}}: \code{NULL} or a numeric matrix with one row per embedded
work, named by its \code{work_id}, and one column per dimension. Works may
have no row: works without text to embed (\code{\link[=sm_embed_works]{sm_embed_works()}}), works
missing from a cache (\code{\link[=sm_embed_load]{sm_embed_load()}}) or works for which a service
has no vector (\code{\link[=sm_enrich_specter]{sm_enrich_specter()}}). The clustering functions give
them no cluster (\code{NA}), and \code{\link[=sm_network_semantic]{sm_network_semantic()}} and
\code{\link[=sm_plot_landscape]{sm_plot_landscape()}} leave them out. \code{metadata$embedding} records the
model (see below).

\strong{\code{provenance}}: one row per work and source. Required:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{work_id} \tab character \tab The work \cr
   \code{source} \tab character \tab Where the record comes from, such as \code{"wos"} \cr
   \code{source_id_external} \tab character \tab Its identifier at the source \cr
   \code{fetch_date} \tab POSIXct \tab When it was read or fetched \cr
   \code{query} \tab character \tab The query or file it came from \cr
   \code{engine} \tab character \tab The parser or client that read it \cr
   \code{scimapR_version} \tab character \tab The scimapR version that read it \cr
   \code{prompt_hash} \tab character \tab Hash of a prompt behind the record \cr
}


\code{source_id_external} is, for example, the Web of Science accession
number (UT); \code{prompt_hash} is \code{NA} for records that no prompt
produced. Optional:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{kind} \tab character \tab The kind of step that wrote the row (see below) \cr
   \code{filter} \tab character \tab The filter of a search, else \code{NA} \cr
   \code{n_max} \tab integer \tab The maximum number of works of a search \cr
}


\code{kind} is \code{"api_query"} for the searches of \code{\link[=sm_fetch_openalex]{sm_fetch_openalex()}},
\code{\link[=sm_fetch_pubmed]{sm_fetch_pubmed()}}, \code{\link[=sm_fetch_crossref]{sm_fetch_crossref()}} and
\code{\link[=sm_corpus_for_question]{sm_corpus_for_question()}}, which also write \code{filter} and \code{n_max};
\code{"file"} for the file readers, whose \code{query} holds the file relative to
the working directory when it lies below it, else its name; and
\code{"enrichment"} for the enrichers, such as \code{\link[=sm_enrich_openalex_impact]{sm_enrich_openalex_impact()}},
whose rows have the source \code{"openalex_impact"}. \code{\link[=sm_certificate]{sm_certificate()}}
records the kind of each step, inferred from the source, query and
engine for rows without \code{kind}, and \code{\link[=sm_rebuild_from_cert]{sm_rebuild_from_cert()}} replays
only searches, never a file read or an enrichment.

\strong{\code{screening}}: one row per screening decision. Required:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{work_id} \tab character \tab The work \cr
   \code{stage} \tab character \tab Stage, such as \code{"title-abstract"} or \code{"full-text"} \cr
   \code{decision} \tab character \tab \code{"include"}, \code{"exclude"} or \code{"uncertain"} \cr
   \code{reason} \tab character \tab Reason for the decision \cr
   \code{confidence} \tab double \tab Confidence of the decision \cr
   \code{source} \tab character \tab Who or what decided \cr
   \code{decided_at} \tab POSIXct \tab When \cr
}


Optional: \code{model} records the chat-provider model for the decision.
The legacy decision \code{"unclear"} is read as \code{"uncertain"}.

\strong{\code{citations_by_year}}: optional (\code{NULL} when the corpus has no yearly
citation counts); one row per work and citation year, as the OpenAlex
readers, \code{\link[=sm_enrich_openalex_impact]{sm_enrich_openalex_impact()}} and \code{\link[=sm_corpus_from_tables]{sm_corpus_from_tables()}}
write it:\tabular{lll}{
   Column \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{work_id} \tab character \tab The cited work \cr
   \code{year} \tab integer \tab The year of the citations \cr
   \code{cited_by_count} \tab integer \tab Citations received in that year, 0 or more \cr
}


\code{work_id} and \code{year} may not be missing, and each pair appears once.
OpenAlex gives the counts of the ten most recent years and leaves out
the years without citations, so a missing year counts 0 for a work with
yearly counts (see \code{works$citations_as_of} and \code{\link[=sm_citation_window]{sm_citation_window()}}).
Subsetting keeps the rows of the kept works, \code{\link[=sm_bind_corpora]{sm_bind_corpora()}} binds
them and \code{\link[=sm_hash_corpus]{sm_hash_corpus()}} includes them when there are any.

\strong{\code{metadata}}: a list. Entries are required unless marked optional:\tabular{lll}{
   Entry \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{scimapR_version} \tab character \tab The scimapR version that built the corpus \cr
   \code{build_date} \tab POSIXct \tab When the corpus was built \cr
   \code{corpus_hash} \tab character \tab Hash of the content (\code{\link[=sm_hash_corpus]{sm_hash_corpus()}}) \cr
   \code{is_locked} \tab logical \tab Whether \code{\link[=sm_lock]{sm_lock()}} locked the corpus \cr
   \code{last_refresh} \tab POSIXct \tab Last refresh without a failed request \cr
   \code{n_identified} \tab integer \tab Optional count before search deduplication \cr
   \code{question_id} \tab character \tab Id of its \code{\link[=sm_question]{sm_question()}} \cr
   \code{fetch_incomplete} \tab list \tab Optional failed-fetch report (see below) \cr
}


\code{n_identified} includes records removed by question-search deduplication
or its result limit.

\code{last_refresh} advances only when \code{\link[=sm_refresh]{sm_refresh()}} refreshed at least one
work and no request failed. A recovered partial fetch has optional
\code{fetch_incomplete} (list) with \code{source}, \code{n_fetched}, \code{n_expected} and
\code{reason}; see the fetchers' Failed requests sections.

\code{question_id} is set by \code{\link[=sm_corpus_for_question]{sm_corpus_for_question()}}. Optional entries
\code{locked_at} (POSIXct), \code{lock_reason} (character) and \code{lock_hash}
(character) record when, why and which content \code{\link[=sm_lock]{sm_lock()}} locked.
Subsets remain locked but discard \code{lock_hash}.
Other optional entries include those added by readers, such as
\code{source_file} and \code{reader};
\code{hash_version} (integer), the algorithm of \code{corpus_hash}
(\code{\link[=sm_hash_corpus]{sm_hash_corpus()}}), which \code{\link[=sm_snapshot]{sm_snapshot()}} writes (a snapshot without
it used version 1); and \code{local_inputs}, a tibble of the local files the
corpus was built from, with their SHA-256 hashes, which
\code{\link[=sm_register_input]{sm_register_input()}} and \code{\link[=sm_corpus_from_tables]{sm_corpus_from_tables()}} (\code{inputs}) write,
\code{\link[=sm_certificate]{sm_certificate()}} records and \code{\link[=sm_verify_certificate]{sm_verify_certificate()}} checks (see
\code{\link[=sm_register_input]{sm_register_input()}} for its columns).
\code{build_date} and \code{last_refresh} may also be numbers of seconds since
1970-01-01 UTC, as corpora restored from tables store them, ISO 8601
text or dates; \code{\link[=sm_certificate]{sm_certificate()}} and the citation-maturity cut-offs
read all of these.

Optional: \code{embedding}, the record of the model behind \code{embeddings}, a
list that \code{\link[=sm_embed_works]{sm_embed_works()}}, \code{\link[=sm_enrich_specter]{sm_enrich_specter()}},
\code{\link[=sm_fetch_semantic_scholar]{sm_fetch_semantic_scholar()}}, \code{\link[=sm_embed_load]{sm_embed_load()}} (from the cache of
\code{\link[=sm_embed_save]{sm_embed_save()}}) and \code{\link[=sm_bind_corpora]{sm_bind_corpora()}} write and
\code{\link[=sm_certificate]{sm_certificate()}} reports:\tabular{lll}{
   Entry \tab Type \tab Content \cr
   \code{model} \tab character \tab Short model name, \code{"mixed"} for several \cr
   \code{model_id} \tab character \tab Hugging Face id or Semantic Scholar field \cr
   \code{base_model_id} \tab character \tab Base model of an adapter, else \code{NA} \cr
   \code{revision} \tab character \tab Commit of the model snapshot, or \code{NA} \cr
   \code{base_revision} \tab character \tab Commit of the base model, or \code{NA} \cr
   \code{backend} \tab character \tab The Python package or the service used \cr
   \code{text} \tab character \tab The text embedded, such as \code{"title_abstract"} \cr
   \code{prefix} \tab character \tab Input prefix, else \code{""} \cr
   \code{versions} \tab list \tab Versions of the Python packages used \cr
   \code{models} \tab character \tab The model ids behind the rows \cr
   \code{n_works}, \code{n_dimensions} \tab integer \tab Size of the matrix when made \cr
   \code{created} \tab POSIXct \tab When the record was written (UTC) \cr
}


\code{model_id} is the Hugging Face id of the model (for \code{"specter2"} that
of the adapter, whose base model is \code{base_model_id}) or the Semantic
Scholar field of the vectors (\code{"embedding.specter_v2"} or
\code{"embedding.specter_v1"}), whose \code{revision} is the model version that
Semantic Scholar reports. \code{backend} is \code{"sentence-transformers"},
\code{"adapters"}, \code{"semantic-scholar"} or \code{"mixed"}; a mix lists its models
in \code{models}. \code{prefix} is \code{"query: "} for \code{"multilingual-e5"}.
A record whose \code{n_dimensions} differs from the width of \code{embeddings},
or whose \code{n_works} is smaller than its number of rows, no longer
describes the matrix and counts as no record. Embeddings without a
record, such as those made by earlier versions of scimapR, have a model
that is not recorded: \code{\link[=sm_certificate]{sm_certificate()}} reports it as \code{"unknown"}, and
the functions that combine embeddings combine them with recorded ones
only when asked to (\code{mix_models}).
}
\examples{
corpus <- sm_corpus(
  works = tibble::tibble(
    work_id = "W000000001",
    doi = "10.1234/example",
    title = "Example Work",
    abstract = "An example abstract.",
    year = 2024L,
    type = "journal-article",
    source_id = NA_character_,
    cited_by_count = 0L,
    oa_status = "closed",
    language = "en",
    pmid = NA_character_,
    arxiv_id = NA_character_,
    openalex_id = NA_character_,
    is_retracted = FALSE,
    retraction_date = NA_real_,
    last_refreshed = Sys.time()
  )
)
print(corpus)
}
